    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Pullman-WA/Animal-Technician-1_R-15240": {
      "title": "Animal Technician 1",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: September 24, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 525E-YN_CS_NPS - Animal Technician 1 Business Title: Animal Technician 1 Employee Type: Classified Position Details: The Opportunity: As an Animal Technician 1 , you will work as part of a dedicated animal care team to perform routine tasks related to the care, monitoring, handling, and feeding of animals, as well as custodial duties within multiple university animal facilities. This role will require you to follow detailed instructions, communicate effectively, and maintain strong attention to detail in all aspects of animal care. You will support vivarium users within the Integrated Vivarium Services Unit (IVS) at WSU Pullman. You will be responsible for periodically driving distances to pick up animal shipments from regional airports. i.e. Pullman, Lewiston, or Spokane. This is an essential position, and you will be expected to report to work under conditions that other employees are not required to report to work, such as inclement weather and   WSU   suspended operations. The Office of the Campus Veterinarian supports humane and ethical care and treatment of animals involved in research and education at WSU. The OCV Integrated Vivarium Services provides daily care of animals in several facilities located on the WSU Pullman campus. The professional animal care technicians provide care, custodial support, husbandry, technical services and research support. This recruitment is for a 100% FTE (fulltime permanent) position. Expectation on hours to be worked for this position would be Monday - Friday; between the hours of 7 AM - 5 PM. Weekend and Holiday hours on a rotating bases is also required. To support WSU’s ability to attract talent in a competitive job market, this recruitment has been approved for a hiring incentive payment of up to $1,500.00. More information regarding the hiring incentive payment program can be located on the HRS website: Hiring Incentive – Human Resource Services, Washington State University (wsu.edu) . Additional Information: This is a full-time ( 100% FTE), permanent position. This position is overtime eligible. Monthly Salary: $3,195 to $4,201 | Range 34 (Steps A-M) | Successful candidates are typically hired at the beginning of the salary range and receive scheduled salary increment increases in accordance with WAC 357-28 . In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts; training and development; and employee tuition waiver. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Classified staff and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Physical ability to do required work; ability to read and write. 18 years of age or older. High school diploma or equivalent. Must have, or be able to obtain by time of hire, a valid driver’s license and meet requirements in accordance with SPPM 7.10 and departmental driving standards. Must be able to travel, drive distance, to pick up animal shipments from airports. i.e. Pullman, Lewiston, or Spokane. Must be able to drive a 1-ton lift gate truck with manual transmission. Must be able to wear respiratory protective equipment, if necessary, based on research needs, occupational health and safety requirements, and requirements set by WSU bio-safety officer. Must abstain from wearing scented products or using scented products at work due to the sensitivity of the animals and research. Must be able to work a rotating schedule that could include weekends, after hours and holidays as needed and directed by supervisor. This is an essential position, and the employee will be expected to report to work under conditions that other employees are not required to report to work, such as inclement weather and WSU suspended operations. Preferred Qualifications: Prior husbandry experience with a wide range of animal species and working with animals in a research or teaching institution. Experience managing rodent breeding colonies AALAS Certification at the Assistant Laboratory Animal Technician level or higher. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship. About The Office of the Campus Veterinarian - Office of the Campus Veterinarian Area/College: Office of Research - Area 35   Department Name: Office of the Campus Veterinarian   Location: WSU Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Resume 2) Cover Letter External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Position Term: 12 Month Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Pullman-WA/International-Admissions-Prospect-Coordinator_R-15364": {
      "title": "International Admissions Prospect Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: September 27, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 1481-YN_ADMINPRO - Student Services Coordinator/Advisor 1 Business Title: International Admissions Prospect Coordinator Employee Type: Admin. Professional Position Details: The Opportunity: The International Admissions Prospect Coordinator is responsible for performing specialized technical/clerical duties in support of the Office of International Programs Admissions and Recruitment unit. Under general supervision of the Assistant Director this individual will provide students, staff, and program participants as well as the public with information and interpretation of policies and activities related to the programs specialty. The individual will compose written communications, establish and maintain records relating to the day-to-day operation of the admissions and recruitment unit. The incumbent will work closely with the Assistant Director for International Admissions Processing and Evaluation to assist prospective international students with educational opportunities and to understand and to successfully complete Washington State University’s application requirements. Primary responsibility will be to manage the inquiry, application start and submission/completion stage of the admissions funnel, as well as supporting the evaluation and admission offer stage.  The incumbent must have cross-cultural sensitivities that allow clear communication with international applicants, current international students, and other stakeholders. The individual will evaluate international first-year, transfer, non-degree and former student undergraduate admission applications, and Academic English (ESL) admissions applications. The International Admissions Prospect Coordinator reviews international academic documents in order to make appropriate admission decisions for all international applicants. Responsibilities include: basic understanding of international student immigration rules and government databases; ability to analyze and process international applications; evaluate transfer credit and international/domestic transcripts; assess appropriate academic levels for the students, and respond to inquiries. Additional Information: This is a full time (100 % FTE), permanent position. This position is overtime eligible. Monthly Salary: $3,750.85 - $3,838.00 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts; training and development; and employee tuition waiver. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for AP staff and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Positions require a Bachelor’s degree and two (2) years of professional work experience in student services or related education/experience. A Master’s degree in a related field may substitute for one (1) year of professional work experience. Any combination of relevant education and professional experience may be substituted for the educational requirement on a year‐for‐year basis. US Citizen or Permanent Resident ( SEVP   eligibility for becoming a Designated School Official   DSO ). Experience in international student services and/or admissions. Preferred Qualifications: Strong interpersonal and organizational skills. Experience that demonstrates the ability to work with a diverse student population and knowledge of multicultural issues. Experience with the Student and Exchange Visitor Information System ( SEVIS ). Experience advising students. Experience conducting recruitment activities. Experience researching, interpreting and applying regulations and policies. Experience working with multicultural and/or international student populations. Experience using Slate or another   CRM   system.​ Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - WSU International | Washington State University Area/College: Office of International Programs   Department Name: International Admissions and Recruitment   Location: Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Resume 2) Cover Letter External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Position Term: 12 Month Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Transmission-of-Metabolic-Disease-and-Translational-Biotechnology---Tenure-Track_R-15369": {
      "title": "Transmission of Metabolic Disease and Translational Biotechnology | Tenure Track",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 1, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor, 280-NN_FACULTY - Associate Professor Business Title: Transmission of Metabolic Disease and Translational Biotechnology | Tenure Track Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Molecular Biosciences (SMB) at Washington State University (WSU) invites applications for two full-time, 12-month, permanent tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant and/or Associate Professor, depending on qualifications. The positions will be filled through one coordinated search. Across both positions, the principal considerations will be scientific excellence, the importance of the problems addressed, the ability to fill a genuine expertise or capability gap, complementarity with existing faculty, and contributions to research, teaching, mentoring, collaborative funding, and interdisciplinary program development. Each position is expected to be approximately 65% research, 25% teaching and mentoring, and 10% service, with final effort distribution negotiable and determined at the time of offer. Successful candidates will establish or maintain innovative, rigorous, and extramurally funded research programs; publish influential scholarship; mentor undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral trainees; and contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction. Teaching may include molecular and cellular biology, genetics, genomics, epigenetics, developmental or reproductive biology, metabolism, biotechnology, bioengineering, computational biology, bioinformatics, quantitative reasoning, or related areas. Successful candidates will also contribute to interdisciplinary grants, centers, shared platforms, training programs, technology development, and academic service. Associate Professor appointees will provide additional intellectual and programmatic leadership appropriate to rank. Applicants may align primarily with either of the following complementary themes or contribute across both. THEME 1 - TRANSMISSION OF METABOLIC DISORDERS ACROSS GENERATIONS: SMB seeks candidates whose research investigates how environmental, lifestyle, and biological factors influence metabolic health and disease across generations. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, the genetic and epigenetic mechanisms underlying developmental programming, metabolic disease susceptibility, and intergenerational/transgenerational epigenetic inheritance. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary team focused on elucidating the developmental and germline origins of chronic and metabolic diseases, including obesity, diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, neurodegenerative disorders, reproductive aging, and related conditions. Through collaborative and integrative research, the candidate will contribute to advancing fundamental understanding of disease etiology and identifying new opportunities for prevention and intervention. This research theme is a major focus of the Center for Lifestyle, Environment, Epigenetics, and Reproductive Health (CLEERH), and the successful candidate is expected to serve as a core contributor to the CLEERH's research, training, and outreach activities. THEME 2 - TRANSLATIONAL BIOTECHNOLOGY AND ENABLING PLATFORMS: SMB seeks candidates who develop innovative technologies and/or platforms that move biological discoveries toward practical applications. Relevant areas include nanobiotechnology; advanced drug or biologic delivery; precision gene editing, including base or prime editing; protein or genetic engineering; high-throughput screening; biosensors and diagnostics; tissue or organ engineering; biomaterials; and related translational technologies. Preferred candidates will have a demonstrated capacity to build and lead a highly collaborative research program that spans multiple research groups within SMB, fostering partnerships that accelerate discovery, translation, therapeutic and diagnostic development, biotechnology innovation, and commercialization. Additional Information: The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or another mutually agreed-upon date. Annual 12-Month Salary: Assistant Professor $130,000–$145,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications Associate Professor $150,000–$170,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications for all ranks: Doctoral degree (Ph.D., M.D., D.V.M., or equivalent) in a relevant biological, biomedical, computational, quantitative, or related field is required by the time of hire. Demonstrate the ability to conduct rigorous research, publish influential peer-reviewed work, collaborate effectively, and contribute to high-quality undergraduate and graduate education. Required Qualifications for Assistant Professor: Demonstrate a strong record of research accomplishment and clear potential to establish an innovative, and extramurally funded research program. Required Qualifications for Associate Professor: Established record of high-quality scholarship, sustained or ongoing extramural funding, effective mentoring, and national or international recognition appropriate for rank and tenure. Candidates must also demonstrate the ability to work collegially with faculty, staff, and students from a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. For more information on the College of Veterinary Medicine’s tenure and promotion guidelines, please see here . Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to candidates whose research aligns with one or both themes and adds distinctive, strategically important, and complementary expertise to SMB. Preferred candidates will address a clearly defined and consequential problem; bring missing expertise or establish an enabling platform that strengthens multiple groups; use rigorous molecular, cellular, genetic, genomic, epigenomic, physiological, computational, engineering, imaging, or systems-level approaches; and demonstrate potential for collaborative, programmatic, center-level, training-grant, shared-platform, or translational initiatives. Intergenerational health candidates should explain how their work connects germline transmission, the environment or lifestyle, and chronic or metabolic disease. Translational-biotechnology candidates should explain how their expertise and skillsets can enable unique discoveries and advance translation across multiple biological areas. Experience or interest in intellectual property, industry partnerships, commercialization, or translation into clinical, veterinary, agricultural, or public health applications is desirable. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About SMB: Washington State University's School of Molecular Biosciences (SMB), within the College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM), is working to solve some of societies’ biggest challenges by building highly collaborative multidisciplinary teams anchored to problem-based investigation. Existing strengths include expertise in germ cell and reproductive tract biology, skin biology, DNA repair and chromatin biology, epigenetics, animal biotechnology, microbiology, and immunology. SMB fosters a collaborative, intellectually curious, and interdisciplinary environment committed to scientific excellence. Faculty are encouraged to take initiative, mentor trainees, engage across fields, and build productive collaborations. The School seeks to strengthen the translational impact of fundamental discovery through new technologies, diagnostics, therapeutics, and other meaningful applications. Additional information is available at https://smb.wsu.edu/ . Area/College: College of Veterinary Medicine Department Name: School of Molecular Biosciences Location: Pullman, WA Screening Date: Applicant screening will begin October 26, 2026 Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1 ) Cover letter identifying the rank sought and relevant theme or themes; 2) Curriculum vitae; 3) Statement of research accomplishments and future plans; 4) Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy and interests Applicants should explain the problem their research addresses, the expertise or enabling capability they would add to SMB, how their work complements existing faculty, and how they would contribute to interdisciplinary research, education, and translation. References may be contacted later in the recruitment process. Questions may be directed to Laurilee Kramer at lkramer@wsu.edu . External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Pullman-WA/Clinical-Pathology---Clinical-Career-Track---Open-Rank_R-15325": {
      "title": "Clinical Pathology | Clinical Career Track | Open Rank",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: October 4, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 164-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Assistant Professor - Career Business Title: Clinical Pathology | Clinical Career Track | Open Rank Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The Washington State University (WSU) Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences (VCS) in the College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) seeks to fill a full-time continuous career track (clinical sub-track) A ssistant or Associate Professor faculty position within the Clinical Pathology section of the Veterinary Teaching Hospital (VTH). The position is available immediately. The successful applicant will be expected to provide diagnostic service for the VTH; clinical and didactic instruction of professional veterinary students, interns, residents, and graduate students. The opportunity to conduct research/scholarship within the candidate’s area of interest is available. The distribution of effort between clinical service, teaching, scholarly effort and academic service/outreach is negotiable. The successful applicant must be able to effectively communicate and work with people of diverse backgrounds The successful applicant will join one to two other full-time board-certified clinical pathologists and a dedicated team of two residents, one laboratory manager, two certified medical laboratory scientists, two laboratory assistants, and multiple, trained, DVM student employees. The well-equipped Clinical Pathology Laboratory ( https://hospital.vetmed.wsu.edu/hospital-wide-services/clinical-pathology/ ) processes over 15,000 patient samples per year. Additional Information: Annual Salary: Assistant Professor: $145,000 - $152,000 Associate Professor: $160,000 - $170,000 In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Qualifications for all ranks: Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM, VMD) degree or a related foreign equivalent degree (such as a BVSc) Board-certified or board-eligible in Clinical Pathology by the American College of Veterinary Pathology or the European College of Veterinary Pathology by the time of hire. Considerations regarding faculty rank will be determined based on the interests and qualifications of the candidate meeting CVM’s promotion guidelines, as will division of effort. Candidates for Associate Professor should have evidence of progressively higher levels of experience and expertise in the provision of outstanding clinical service, clinical teaching, and/or research/scholarship. For more information, see the CVM’s promotion guidelines: ( https://provost.wsu.edu/documents/2023/03/vet-med-promotion-and-tenure-guidelines.pdf ).  Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Eligible for work visa sponsorship Application Information: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Cover letter summarizing the candidate’s qualifications, stating the desired faculty rank 2) Summary of the candidate’s clinical, teaching, scholarly, and outreach and community engagement interests and goals 3) Curriculum vitae Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Jillian Haines, Chair of the Search Committee, by email ( jmhaines@wsu.edu ) or by telephone (509) 335-0711.   Area Overview: The WSU CVM is organized into five departments/schools, including VCS, Veterinary Microbiology and Pathology (VMP), Integrative Physiology and Neuroscience (IPN), the School of Molecular Biosciences (SMB), and the Paul G. Allen School for Global Health, each dedicated to excellence in teaching, research, and service ( http://www.vetmed.wsu.edu ). We are also home to a tier-one Veterinary National Laboratory (Washington Animal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory ( https://waddl.vetmed.wsu.edu/ ) with multiple board-certified anatomic pathologists and microbiologists. The VTH is a full-service referral and primary care center that aspires to be the country’s best academic veterinary hospital for patients, students, clients, colleagues, and community. The joint mission of the VTH and VCS is to improve the health and well-being of all animals through excellence in medical care, training future veterinarians, and finding innovative ways to diagnose and treat disease. Dedicated to this vision and mission are more than 60 full-time faculty members and 170 staff and house officers. The VTH is a leading referral center in the state of Washington and the Pacific Northwest with over 20,000 patient visits per year as a regional tertiary care facility which provides the Clinical Pathology service with a broad-based caseload. The Clinical Pathology Laboratory provides clinical diagnostic services and professional laboratory expertise nationally and internationally to veterinary hospitals, referring veterinarians, and referring laboratories. Our services include state of the art automated hematology, coagulation, clinical chemistry, blood-gas, and immunoassay analyzers as well as fluid analysis, cytology, and urinalysis which are performed by certified medical laboratory scientists and clinical pathology trained veterinary medicine students. Diagnostic services are provided for multiple species of animals including equine, bovine, canine, feline, exotics, camelids, caprine and others. In addition to services provided on site, services are provided nationally via overnight mail which can be utilized by our clients and customers for comprehensive testing. The clinical pathology laboratory is dedicated to educating current and future veterinarians through pre-clinical teaching, clinical rotations, and residency and graduate student training ( https://vetmed.wsu.edu/education/residencies-internships/combined-clinical-pathology-residency/ ) as well as providing services for faculty and resident research projects. WSU Overview: Washington State University is a land-grant institution with an enrollment of approximately 20,000 students on the Pullman campus and 23,000 students system-wide.  The University of Idaho, also a land-grant university, is located in Moscow, Idaho, 8 miles from the Pullman campus.  These communities offer a friendly, small-town living environment and access to a rich assortment of cultural, recreational, and athletic attractions.  Pullman has a four-season climate, clean air, good schools, and a low crime rate.  Pullman (population 34,000) is about 75 miles south of Spokane, Washington, and lies close to recreational areas in Washington, Oregon, Idaho, Montana, and British Columbia. To learn more about the Pullman community, visit:  http://www.pullmanchamber.com . The university’s web page, www.wsu.edu , offers additional information about WSU and the surrounding area. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Pullman-WA/Finance-Budget-Manager_R-15345": {
      "title": "Finance/Budget Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: September 20, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 1143-NN_ADMINPRO - Finance/Budget Manager, 1143-YN_ADMINPRO - Finance/Budget Manager Business Title: Finance/Budget Manager Employee Type: Admin. Professional Position Details: The Opportunity: As the Finance/Budget Manager , you will serve as the lead non-faculty administrator for the Department of Crop and Soil Sciences, a diverse academic and research department with more than 30 faculty programs. You will be responsible for the fiscal, budgetary, administrative, and operational management of the department and will serve as a principal advisor to the Chair on fiscal, personnel, operational, and administrative matters. In this role, you will coordinate and oversee departmental financial resources, including operating, grant, contract, gift, and other funding sources. You will supervise administrative support staff, ensure compliance with university, state, federal, and granting agency policies and regulations, and collaborate with departmental, college, university, and external partners, including USDA-ARS units, to support the effective administration and overall functions of the department. Additional Information: This is a full time ( 100% FTE), permanent position. Overtime eligibility will be determined based on final salary placement.   Monthly Salary: $4,942.10 - $7,569.55 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts; training and development; and employee tuition waiver. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for AP staff and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in a related field, and four (4) years of experience directly related to the duties and responsibilities specified which has included at least one (1) year of supervisory experience. Any combination of relevant education and experience may be substituted for the educational requirement on a year-for-year basis. Demonstrated experience managing administrative, operational, fiscal, or business functions.  Demonstrated experience using Microsoft Excel and/or other computer software applications for reporting, analysis, and record keeping. Demonstrated experience developing, monitoring, reconciling, and analyzing budgets and financial reports. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively orally and in writing. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply policies, procedures, rules, and regulations.  Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated experience using computerized accounting, financial, HR, and/or administrative information systems. Experience with state and government regulations pertaining to fiscal management.  Experience working in an academic environment.  Experience working with grants, contracts, cooperative agreements, and sponsored project funding.  Related professional experience at WSU or another higher education institutions.  Demonstrated experience with short and long-term planning, or academic program administration.  Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - https://css.wsu.edu/ Area/College: College of Agricultural, Human and Natural Resource Sciences (CAHNRS) Department Name: Department of Crop and Soil Sciences Location: Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Resume 2) Cover Letter External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Position Term: 12 Month Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
